\frac{x}{8\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074986/state.json" "file_sha256" "98cd9a0d00664aad6bd9b61b1f95ba7af238532b160bbb9c07501e577ada2b6a"} {"question" "q-343905" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the differential equation ${y}^{″}+\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}}{9}y=f(x),$ where the $6$-periodic forcing function is given by \n\n$$\nf(x)∼-3+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{5}{{n}^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below regarding the resonant component of a particular solution of the equation.\n\nThe natural frequency of the system matches the frequency of the {{field-1}} harmonic of the forcing function. \n\nConsequently, the particular solution contains a resonant term of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{2}}={{field-2}}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074986/state.json" "file_sha256" "98cd9a0d00664aad6bd9b61b1f95ba7af238532b160bbb9c07501e577ada2b6a"} {"question" "q-343905" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We begin by identifying resonance. The differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}}{9}y=f(x)\n$$\n\nhas a natural frequency of \n\n$$\n\\omega =\\sqrt{\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}}{9}}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nThe frequencies of the forcing harmonics for a $6$-periodic function $(L=3)$ are \n\n$$\n{\\omega }_{n}=\\frac{n\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nFor $n=2$, we have ${\\omega }_{2}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.$ Since ${\\omega }_{2}=\\omega ,$ the $n=2$ harmonic of the forcing function causes resonance.\n\nFor the $n=2$ harmonic, the forcing term is \n\n$$\n{f}_{2}(x)=\\frac{5}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nBecause this frequency matches the natural frequency, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{2}}=x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{y}_{{p}_{2}}^{′} & =(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}))+x(-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{2\\pi }{3}B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})) \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{y}_{{p}_{2}}^{″} & =-\\frac{4\\pi }{3}A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{4\\pi }{3}B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})-\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}}{9}x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{{p}_{2}}^{″}$ and ${y}_{{p}_{2}}$ into the equation ${y}^{″}+\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}}{9}y=\\frac{5}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}):$\n\n$$\n[-\\frac{4\\pi }{3}A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{4\\pi }{3}B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})-\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}}{9}{y}_{{p}_{2}}]+\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}}{9}{y}_{{p}_{2}}=\\frac{5}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})\n$$\n\nThe resonant terms involving $x$ cancel out, leaving\n\n$$\n-\\frac{4\\pi }{3}A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{4\\pi }{3}B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})=\\frac{5}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the sine and cosine coefficients, we obtain:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4\\pi }{3}A=\\frac{5}{4} \\\\ \\frac{4\\pi }{3}B=0\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}A=-\\frac{15}{16\\pi } \\\\ B=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resonant component of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{2}}=-\\frac{15x}{16\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074986/state.json" "file_sha256" "98cd9a0d00664aad6bd9b61b1f95ba7af238532b160bbb9c07501e577ada2b6a"} {"question" "q-343905" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074986/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "780dcc0827b7cc928c082b0b4b7b45ad18ea474c27524d9603b01ad71c22a1bf"} {"question" "q-343905" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074986/state.json" "file_sha256" "98cd9a0d00664aad6bd9b61b1f95ba7af238532b160bbb9c07501e577ada2b6a"} {"question" "q-343905" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "n=1"] ["math" "n=2"] ["math" "n=3"] ["math" "n=4"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074986/state.json" "file_sha256" "98cd9a0d00664aad6bd9b61b1f95ba7af238532b160bbb9c07501e577ada2b6a"} {"question" "q-343905" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "n=2"] "category" "ma_explicit_answer" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074986/state.json" "file_sha256" "98cd9a0d00664aad6bd9b61b1f95ba7af238532b160bbb9c07501e577ada2b6a"} {"question" "q-343905" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074986/state.json" "file_sha256" "98cd9a0d00664aad6bd9b61b1f95ba7af238532b160bbb9c07501e577ada2b6a"} {"question" "q-343905" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{15x}{16\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})"] ["math" "-\\frac{15x}{32\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})"] ["math" "-\\frac{15x}{8\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})"] ["math" "\\frac{15x}{16\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074986/state.json" "file_sha256" "98cd9a0d00664aad6bd9b61b1f95ba7af238532b160bbb9c07501e577ada2b6a"} {"question" "q-343905" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{15x}{16\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})"] "category" "ma_explicit_answer" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074986/state.json" "file_sha256" "98cd9a0d00664aad6bd9b61b1f95ba7af238532b160bbb9c07501e577ada2b6a"} {"question" "q-343891" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the differential equation ${y}^{″}+36{\\pi }^{2}y=f(x),$ where the $1$-periodic forcing function is given by \n\n$$\nf(x)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below regarding the resonant component of a particular solution of the equation.\n\nThe natural frequency of the system matches the frequency of the {{field-1}} harmonic of the forcing function. \n\nConsequently, the particular solution contains a resonant term of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{3}}={{field-2}}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074986/state.json" "file_sha256" "98cd9a0d00664aad6bd9b61b1f95ba7af238532b160bbb9c07501e577ada2b6a"} {"question" "q-343891" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We begin by identifying resonance. The differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+36{\\pi }^{2}y=f(x)\n$$\n\nhas a natural frequency of \n\n$$\n\\omega =\\sqrt{36{\\pi }^{2}}=6\\pi .\n$$\n\nThe frequencies of the forcing harmonics for a $1$-periodic function $(L=\\frac{1}{2})$ are \n\n$$\n{\\omega }_{n}=\\frac{n\\pi }{(\\frac{1}{2})}=2n\\pi .\n$$\n\nFor $n=3$, we have ${\\omega }_{3}=6\\pi .$ Since ${\\omega }_{3}=\\omega ,$ the $n=3$ harmonic of the forcing function causes resonance.\n\nFor the $n=3$ harmonic, the forcing term is \n\n$$\n{f}_{3}(x)=\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi x).\n$$\n\nBecause this frequency matches the natural frequency, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{3}}=x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi x)).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{{p}_{3}}^{′} & =(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi x))+x(-6\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi x)+6\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi x)) \\\\ {y}_{{p}_{3}}^{″} & =-12\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi x)+12\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi x)-36{\\pi }^{2}x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi x))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{{p}_{3}}^{″}$ and ${y}_{{p}_{3}}$ into the equation ${y}^{″}+36{\\pi }^{2}y=\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi x):$\n\n$$\n[-12\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi x)+12\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi x)-36{\\pi }^{2}{y}_{{p}_{3}}]+36{\\pi }^{2}{y}_{{p}_{3}}=\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi x)\n$$\n\nThe resonant terms involving $x$ cancel out, leaving\n\n$$\n-12\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi x)+12\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi x)=\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi x).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the sine and cosine coefficients, we obtain:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-12\\pi A=0 \\\\ 12\\pi B=\\frac{1}{9}\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}A=0 \\\\ B=\\frac{1}{108\\pi }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resonant component of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{3}}=\\frac{x}{108\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi x).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074986/state.json" "file_sha256" "98cd9a0d00664aad6bd9b61b1f95ba7af238532b160bbb9c07501e577ada2b6a"} {"question" "q-343891" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074986/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "780dcc0827b7cc928c082b0b4b7b45ad18ea474c27524d9603b01ad71c22a1bf"} {"question" "q-343891" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074986/state.json" "file_sha256" "98cd9a0d00664aad6bd9b61b1f95ba7af238532b160bbb9c07501e577ada2b6a"} {"question" "q-343891" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "n=1"] ["math" "n=2"] ["math" "n=3"] ["math" "n=4"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074986/state.json" "file_sha256" "98cd9a0d00664aad6bd9b61b1f95ba7af238532b160bbb9c07501e577ada2b6a"} {"question" "q-343891" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "n=3"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074986/state.json" "file_sha256" "98cd9a0d00664aad6bd9b61b1f95ba7af238532b160bbb9c07501e577ada2b6a"} {"question" "q-343891" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074986/state.json" "file_sha256" "98cd9a0d00664aad6bd9b61b1f95ba7af238532b160bbb9c07501e577ada2b6a"} {"question" "q-343891" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{x}{108\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi x)"] ["math" "\\frac{x}{108\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi x)"] ["math" "\\frac{x}{216\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi x)"] ["math" "\\frac{x}{54\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi x)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074986/state.json" "file_sha256" "98cd9a0d00664aad6bd9b61b1f95ba7af238532b160bbb9c07501e577ada2b6a"} {"question" "q-343891" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{x}{108\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi x)"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074986/state.json" "file_sha256" "98cd9a0d00664aad6bd9b61b1f95ba7af238532b160bbb9c07501e577ada2b6a"} {"question" "q-343901" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the differential equation ${y}^{″}+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}y=f(x),$ where the $4$-periodic forcing function is given by \n\n$$\nf(x)∼-2+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{6}{{n}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below regarding the resonant component of a particular solution of the equation.\n\nThe natural frequency of the system matches the frequency of the {{field-1}} harmonic of the forcing function. \n\nConsequently, the particular solution contains a resonant term of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{1}}={{field-2}}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074986/state.json" "file_sha256" "98cd9a0d00664aad6bd9b61b1f95ba7af238532b160bbb9c07501e577ada2b6a"} {"question" "q-343901" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We begin by identifying resonance. The differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}y=f(x)\n$$\n\nhas a natural frequency of \n\n$$\n\\omega =\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nThe frequencies of the forcing harmonics for a $4$-periodic function $(L=2)$ are \n\n$$\n{\\omega }_{n}=\\frac{n\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nFor $n=1$, we have ${\\omega }_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Since ${\\omega }_{1}=\\omega ,$ the $n=1$ harmonic of the forcing function causes resonance.\n\nFor the $n=1$ harmonic, the forcing term is \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x)=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}).\n$$\n\nBecause this frequency matches the natural frequency, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{1}}=x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{{p}_{1}}^{′} & =(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}))+x(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})+\\frac{\\pi }{2}B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})) \\\\ {y}_{{p}_{1}}^{″} & =-\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})+\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{{p}_{1}}^{″}$ and ${y}_{{p}_{1}}$ into the equation ${y}^{″}+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}y=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}):$\n\n$$\n[-\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})+\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}{y}_{{p}_{1}}]+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}{y}_{{p}_{1}}=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})\n$$\n\nThe resonant terms involving $x$ cancel out, leaving\n\n$$\n-\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})+\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the sine and cosine coefficients, we obtain:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-\\pi A=0 \\\\ \\pi B=6\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}A=0 \\\\ B=\\frac{6}{\\pi }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resonant component of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{1}}=\\frac{6x}{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074986/state.json" "file_sha256" "98cd9a0d00664aad6bd9b61b1f95ba7af238532b160bbb9c07501e577ada2b6a"} {"question" "q-343901" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074986/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "780dcc0827b7cc928c082b0b4b7b45ad18ea474c27524d9603b01ad71c22a1bf"} {"question" "q-343901" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074986/state.json" "file_sha256" "98cd9a0d00664aad6bd9b61b1f95ba7af238532b160bbb9c07501e577ada2b6a"} {"question" "q-343901" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "n=1"] ["math" "n=2"] ["math" "n=3"] ["math" "n=4"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074986/state.json" "file_sha256" "98cd9a0d00664aad6bd9b61b1f95ba7af238532b160bbb9c07501e577ada2b6a"} {"question" "q-343901" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "n=1"] "category" "ma_explicit_answer" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074986/state.json" "file_sha256" "98cd9a0d00664aad6bd9b61b1f95ba7af238532b160bbb9c07501e577ada2b6a"} {"question" "q-343901" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074986/state.json" "file_sha256" "98cd9a0d00664aad6bd9b61b1f95ba7af238532b160bbb9c07501e577ada2b6a"} {"question" "q-343901" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{6x}{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})"] ["math" "\\frac{12x}{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})"] ["math" "\\frac{3x}{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})"] ["math" "\\frac{6x}{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074986/state.json" "file_sha256" "98cd9a0d00664aad6bd9b61b1f95ba7af238532b160bbb9c07501e577ada2b6a"} {"question" "q-343901" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{6x}{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})"] "category" "ma_explicit_answer" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074986/state.json" "file_sha256" "98cd9a0d00664aad6bd9b61b1f95ba7af238532b160bbb9c07501e577ada2b6a"} {"question" "e-21857" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the Fourier series of a particular solution of the differential equation\n\n$$\ny'-2y=f(x),\n$$\n\nwhere the Fourier series of the $6$-periodic forcing function is given by\n\n$$\nf(x) \\sim 3 + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{12}{n^2\\pi}\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074986/example-21857.json" "file_sha256" "39944069fa3163e8cb39fa4b5baf624ff576f4c2767df660efc183db98140b85"} {"question" "e-21857" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We assume that a particular solution has the following Fourier series:\n\n$$\ny_p \\sim \\dfrac{a_0}{2} + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n \\cos\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right) + b_n \\sin\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})) \\\\  & ∼0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\cdot (-\\frac{n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}))+{b}_{n}\\cdot \\frac{n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\  & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for $y'_p$ and $y_p$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & ({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})) \\\\  & \\,-2(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})) \\\\  & \\,\\,=3+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{12}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRearranging the terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & -{a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}-2{a}_{n})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\  & \\,\\,+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}-2{b}_{n})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,=3+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{12}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, equating the free terms and the coefficients next to sines and cosines, we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-{a}_{0}=3 \\\\ \\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}-2{a}_{n}=\\frac{12}{{n}^{2}\\pi } \\\\ -\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}-2{b}_{n}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation,\n\n$$\na_0=-3,\n$$\n\nwhich means the constant term is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{a_0}{2}=-\\dfrac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nNext, from the third equation,\n$b_n=-\\dfrac{a_n n\\pi}{6}.$\nSubstituting this into the second equation gives\n\n$$\n\\left(-\\dfrac{a_n n\\pi}{6}\\right)\\dfrac{n\\pi}{3}-2a_n=\\dfrac{12}{n^2\\pi}\n\\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad\na_n=-\\dfrac{216}{n^2\\pi(n^2\\pi^2+36)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, for $b_n$ with $n\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{n} & =-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =-\\frac{n\\pi }{6}\\cdot (-\\frac{216}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+36)}) \\\\  & =\\frac{36}{n({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+36)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}∼-\\frac{3}{2} & +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,-\\frac{216}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+36)}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\  & +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{36}{n({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+36)}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074986/example-21857.json" "file_sha256" "39944069fa3163e8cb39fa4b5baf624ff576f4c2767df660efc183db98140b85"} {"question" "e-21858" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the Fourier series of a particular solution of the differential equation $y''-y=f(x),$ where the Fourier series of the $2$-periodic forcing function is given by\n\n$$\nf(x) \\sim 1 + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{(-1)^{n+1}}{n^3\\pi} \\sin(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\n*Hint: We may assume that all the cosine coefficients in the Fourier series of the particular solution are equal to zero.*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074986/example-21858.json" "file_sha256" "dd7108bdd3b7f493ead18e7da9e1d04a35983cf5af8611060b41110c33a6b5c8"} {"question" "e-21858" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Because the forcing function has only a constant term and sine terms, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\ny_p \\sim \\dfrac{a_0}{2} + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty b_n \\sin(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x))∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}n\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x) \\\\ {y}_{p}^{″} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}n\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x))∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{b}_{n}{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for $y''_p$ and $y_p$ into the equation and have\n\n$$\n\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty -b_n n^2\\pi^2 \\sin(n\\pi x)\n-\n\\left( \\dfrac{a_0}{2} + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty b_n \\sin(n\\pi x) \\right)\n=\n1 + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{(-1)^{n+1}}{n^3\\pi} \\sin(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nRearranging the terms, we get\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{a_0}{2} + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty b_n(-n^2\\pi^2-1)\\sin(n\\pi x)\n=\n1 + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{(-1)^{n+1}}{n^3\\pi} \\sin(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the free terms and sine coefficients, we obtain:\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074986/assets/249067c54bdeee50fdddb787db0d9fc6e95eeddd7cbf322cb425c08a2a25d97c.png)\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\ny_p \\sim \n-1\n+ \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\:\n\\dfrac{(-1)^n}{n^3\\pi(n^2\\pi^2+1)}\n\\cdot\n\\sin(n\\pi x).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074986/example-21858.json" "file_sha256" "dd7108bdd3b7f493ead18e7da9e1d04a35983cf5af8611060b41110c33a6b5c8"} {"question" "e-21875" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the differential equation $y'' + 9\\pi^2 y = f(x),$ where the $2$-periodic forcing function is given by\n\n$$\nf(x) \\sim \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{n^2}\\sin(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nWhat harmonic of the forcing function does the natural frequency of the system match?\nAnd what is the resonant component of a particular solution?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074986/example-21875.json" "file_sha256" "6ee3e379ae213ec039e93dcf2c8b021c324fff307282916c44d5919e08bf0cfd"} {"question" "e-21875" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We begin by identifying resonance. The differential equation\n\n$$\ny''+9\\pi^2 y=f(x)\n$$\n\nhas a natural frequency of\n\n$$\n\\omega=\\sqrt{9\\pi^2}=3\\pi.\n$$\n\nThe frequencies of the forcing harmonics for a $2$-periodic function $\\left(L=1\\right)$ are\n\n$$\n\\omega_n=\\dfrac{n\\pi}{1}=n\\pi.\n$$\n\nFor $n=3$, we have $\\omega_3=3\\pi.$ Since $\\omega_3=\\omega,$ the natural frequency of the system matches the frequency of the $n=3$ harmonic of the forcing function. This is the answer to the first question.\n\nFor the $n=3$ harmonic, the forcing term is \n\n$$\nf_3(x)=\\dfrac{1}{9}\\sin(3\\pi x).\n$$\n\nBecause this frequency matches the natural frequency, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\ny_{p_3}=x(A\\cos(3\\pi x)+B\\sin(3\\pi x)).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{{p}_{3}}^{′} & =(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi x))+x(-3\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi x)+3\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi x)) \\\\ {y}_{{p}_{3}}^{″} & =-6\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi x)+6\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi x)-9{\\pi }^{2}x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi x))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for $y''_{p_3}$ and $y_{p_3}$ into the equation $y''+9\\pi^2 y=\\dfrac{1}{9}\\sin(3\\pi x){:}$\n\n$$\n\\left[-6\\pi A\\sin(3\\pi x)+6\\pi B\\cos(3\\pi x)-9\\pi^2 y_{p_3}\\right]+9\\pi^2 y_{p_3}=\\dfrac{1}{9}\\sin(3\\pi x)\n$$\n\nThe resonant terms involving $x$ cancel out, leaving\n\n$$\n-6\\pi A\\sin(3\\pi x)+6\\pi B\\cos(3\\pi x)=\\dfrac{1}{9}\\sin(3\\pi x).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the sine and cosine coefficients, we obtain:\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074986/assets/599de8dda7f476da630651310a373eae36280c75676a44da91105c9a1751456b.png)\n\nTherefore, the resonant component of a particular solution is\n\n$$\ny_{p_3}=-\\dfrac{x}{54\\pi}\\cos(3\\pi x).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074986/example-21875.json" "file_sha256" "6ee3e379ae213ec039e93dcf2c8b021c324fff307282916c44d5919e08bf0cfd"}] "usage" {"e-21858" [] "e-21857" [] "q-343373" [] "q-342229" [] "q-342203" [] "e-21875" []} "basis" 2748 "retractions" [[17592186221968 :question/problem "Select the missing parts in a particular solution of the differential equation $y^{′} - y = f(x)$, where the Fourier series of the $4$-periodic forcing function is given by\n$f(x)∼ - 1 + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)\\frac{4}{n^{2}π}\\cos (\\frac{nπx}{2})$.The Fourier series of a particular solution is\n$1$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}} {{field-3}}"] [17592186221967 :question/problem "Select the missing parts in a particular solution of the differential equation $y^{′} + 5y = f(x)$, where the Fourier series of the $2$-periodic forcing function is given by\n$f(x)∼∑_(n = 1)^(∞)((-1)^{n})/(n^{2}π)\\sin (nπx)$.The Fourier series of a particular solution is\n$- (5(-1)^{n})/(n^{2}π^{2}(n^{2}π^{2} + 25))$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}}"] [17592186221973 :question/problem "Select the missing parts in a particular solution of the differential equation $y^{″} + 16y = f(x)$, where the Fourier series of the $6$-periodic forcing function is given by\n$f(x)∼∑_(n = 1)^(∞)((-1)^{n})/(n^{2}π)\\sin (\\frac{nπx}{3})$.\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the free term and all the cosine coefficients in the Fourier series of the particular solution are equal to zero.*\n\nThe Fourier series of a particular solution is\n$(3(-1)^{n})/(nπ^{2}(4 + nπ))$ {{field-1}}"] [17592186221144 :question/problem "Find the Fourier series of a particular solution of the differential equation\n$y'-2y=f(x)$,\nwhere the Fourier series of the $6$-periodic forcing function is given by\n$f(x) \\sim 3 + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{12}{n^2\\pi}\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)$."] [17592186221144 :question/worked-solution "We assume that a particular solution has the following Fourier series:\n\n$$\ny_p \\sim \\dfrac{a_0}{2} + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n \\cos\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right) + b_n \\sin\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\ny_{p}^{′} \\mid ∼\\frac{d}{dx}(\\frac{a_{0}}{2} + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)a_{n}\\cos (\\frac{nπx}{3}) + b_{n}\\sin (\\frac{nπx}{3})); ∼0 + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)a_{n} \\cdot (-\\frac{nπ}{3}\\sin (\\frac{nπx}{3})) + b_{n} \\cdot \\frac{nπ}{3}\\cos (\\frac{nπx}{3}); ∼∑_(n = 1)^(∞) - \\frac{a_{n}nπ}{3}\\sin (\\frac{nπx}{3}) + \\frac{b_{n}nπ}{3}\\cos (\\frac{nπx}{3})\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for $y'_p$ and $y_p$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n(∑_(n = 1)^(∞) - \\frac{a_{n}nπ}{3}\\sin (\\frac{nπx}{3}) + \\frac{b_{n}nπ}{3}\\cos (\\frac{nπx}{3})); - 2(\\frac{a_{0}}{2} + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)a_{n}\\cos (\\frac{nπx}{3}) + b_{n}\\sin (\\frac{nπx}{3})); = 3 + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)\\frac{12}{n^{2}π}\\cos (\\frac{nπx}{3})\n$$\n\nRearranging the terms, we get\n\n$$\n-a_{0} + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)(\\frac{b_{n}nπ}{3} - 2a_{n})\\cos (\\frac{nπx}{3}); + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)(-\\frac{a_{n}nπ}{3} - 2b_{n})\\sin (\\frac{nπx}{3}); = 3 + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)\\frac{12}{n^{2}π}\\cos (\\frac{nπx}{3})\n$$\n\nNow, equating the free terms and the coefficients next to sines and cosines, we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n{-a_{0} = 3; \\frac{b_{n}nπ}{3} - 2a_{n} = \\frac{12}{n^{2}π}; - \\frac{a_{n}nπ}{3} - 2b_{n} = 0\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation,\n$a_0=-3$,\nwhich means the constant term is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{a_0}{2}=-\\dfrac{3}{2}\n$$\n\nNext, from the third equation,\n\n$$\nb_n=-\\dfrac{a_n n\\pi}{6}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation gives\n\n$$\n\\left(-\\dfrac{a_n n\\pi}{6}\\right)\\dfrac{n\\pi}{3}-2a_n=\\dfrac{12}{n^2\\pi} \\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad a_n=-\\dfrac{216}{n^2\\pi(n^2\\pi^2+36)}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for $b_n$ with\n\n$$\nn\\ge 1\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nb_{n} &= -\\frac{a_{n}nπ}{6} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{nπ}{6} \\cdot (- (216)/(n^{2}π(n^{2}π^{2} + 36))) \\\\\n&= (36)/(n(n^{2}π^{2} + 36))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\ny_{p}∼- 3 2 \\mid + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)- 216 n 2 π (n 2 π 2 + 36) \\cdot \\cos (\\frac{nπx}{3}); + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)36 n (n 2 π 2 + 36) \\cdot \\sin (\\frac{nπx}{3})\n$$"] [17592186221145 :question/problem "Find the Fourier series of a particular solution of the differential equation $y''-y=f(x)$, where the Fourier series of the $2$-periodic forcing function is given by\n$f(x) \\sim 1 + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{(-1)^{n+1}}{n^3\\pi} \\sin(n\\pi x)$.\n\n*Hint: We may assume that all the cosine coefficients in the Fourier series of the particular solution are equal to zero.*"] [17592186221145 :question/worked-solution "Because the forcing function has only a constant term and sine terms, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\ny_p \\sim \\dfrac{a_0}{2} + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty b_n \\sin(n\\pi x)\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}y_{p}^{′} & ∼\\frac{d}{dx}(\\frac{a_{0}}{2} + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)b_{n}\\sin (nπx))∼∑_(n = 1)^(∞)b_{n}nπ\\cos (nπx) \\\\ y_{p}^{″} & ∼\\frac{d}{dx}(∑_(n = 1)^(∞)b_{n}nπ\\cos (nπx))∼∑_(n = 1)^(∞) - b_{n}n^{2}π^{2}\\sin (nπx)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for $y''_p$ and $y_p$ into the equation and have\n\n$$\n\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty -b_n n^2\\pi^2 \\sin(n\\pi x) - \\left(\\dfrac{a_0}{2} + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty b_n \\sin(n\\pi x) \\right) = 1 + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{(-1)^{n+1}}{n^3\\pi} \\sin(n\\pi x)\n$$\n\nRearranging the terms, we get\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{a_0}{2} + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty b_n(-n^2\\pi^2-1)\\sin(n\\pi x) = 1 + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{(-1)^{n+1}}{n^3\\pi} \\sin(n\\pi x)\n$$\n\nNow, equating the free terms and sine coefficients, we obtain:\n\n$$\n{-\\frac{a_{0}}{2} = 1; b_{n}(-n^{2}π^{2} - 1) = ((-1)^{n + 1})/(n^{3}π)\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\ny_p \\sim -1 + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\: \\dfrac{(-1)^n}{n^3\\pi(n^2\\pi^2+1)} \\cdot \\sin(n\\pi x)\n$$"] [17592186221147 :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $y'' + 9\\pi^2 y = f(x)$, where the $2$-periodic forcing function is given by\n$f(x) \\sim \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{n^2}\\sin(n\\pi x)$.\nWhat harmonic of the forcing function does the natural frequency of the system match?\nAnd what is the resonant component of a particular solution?"] [17592186221147 :question/worked-solution "We begin by identifying resonance. The differential equation\n\n$$\ny''+9\\pi^2 y=f(x)\n$$\n\nhas a natural frequency of\n\n$$\n\\omega=\\sqrt{9\\pi^2}=3\\pi\n$$\n\nThe frequencies of the forcing harmonics for a $2$-periodic function\n\n$$\n\\left(L=1\\right)\n$$\n\nare\n\n$$\n\\omega_n=\\dfrac{n\\pi}{1}=n\\pi\n$$\n\nFor $n=3$, we have\n\n$$\n\\omega_3=3\\pi\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\omega_3=\\omega\n$$\n\nthe natural frequency of the system matches the frequency of the $n=3$ harmonic of the forcing function. This is the answer to the first question.\n\nFor the $n=3$ harmonic, the forcing term is\n\n$$\nf_3(x)=\\dfrac{1}{9}\\sin(3\\pi x)\n$$\n\nBecause this frequency matches the natural frequency, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\ny_{p_3}=x(A\\cos(3\\pi x)+B\\sin(3\\pi x))\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{p_{3}}^{′} &= (A\\cos (3πx) + B\\sin (3πx)) + x(-3πA\\sin (3πx) + 3πB\\cos (3πx)) \\\\\ny_{p_{3}}^{″} &= -6πA\\sin (3πx) + 6πB\\cos (3πx) - 9π^{2}x(A\\cos (3πx) + B\\sin (3πx))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for $y''_{p_3}$ and $y_{p_3}$ into the equation\n\n$$\ny''+9\\pi^2 y=\\dfrac{1}{9}\\sin(3\\pi x){:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\left[-6\\pi A\\sin(3\\pi x)+6\\pi B\\cos(3\\pi x)-9\\pi^2 y_{p_3}\\right]+9\\pi^2 y_{p_3}=\\dfrac{1}{9}\\sin(3\\pi x)\n$$\n\nThe resonant terms involving $x$ cancel out, leaving\n\n$$\n-6\\pi A\\sin(3\\pi x)+6\\pi B\\cos(3\\pi x)=\\dfrac{1}{9}\\sin(3\\pi x)\n$$\n\nNow, equating the sine and cosine coefficients, we obtain:\n\n$$\n{-6πA = \\frac{1}{9}; 6πB = 0\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resonant component of a particular solution is\n\n$$\ny_{p_3}=-\\dfrac{x}{54\\pi}\\cos(3\\pi x)\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186220788 17592186220789 17592186220790 17592186220792 17592186220793 17592186220794 17592186220796 17592186220797 17592186220798 17592186220800 17592186220801 17592186220802 17592186220803 17592186220805 17592186220806 17592186220807 17592186220831 17592186220832 17592186220833 17592186220834] "immutable_fields" [[17592186220787 :answer-field/correct] [17592186220787 :answer-field/id] [17592186220787 :answer-field/key] [17592186220787 :answer-field/type] [17592186220791 :answer-field/correct] [17592186220791 :answer-field/id] [17592186220791 :answer-field/key] [17592186220791 :answer-field/type] [17592186220795 :answer-field/correct] [17592186220795 :answer-field/id] [17592186220795 :answer-field/key] [17592186220795 :answer-field/type] [17592186220799 :answer-field/correct] [17592186220799 :answer-field/id] [17592186220799 :answer-field/key] [17592186220799 :answer-field/type] [17592186220804 :answer-field/correct] [17592186220804 :answer-field/id] [17592186220804 :answer-field/key] [17592186220804 :answer-field/type] [17592186220830 :answer-field/correct] [17592186220830 :answer-field/id] [17592186220830 :answer-field/key] [17592186220830 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
